# GeoHub — URL map
#
# IMPORTANT — how Cloudflare Pages already behaves, before any rule here runs:
#   · a file foo.html is served at BOTH /foo and /foo.html
#   · a request for /foo.html is automatically 301'd to /foo
#   · a request for /index.html is automatically 301'd to /
#
# That second line is what matters. A rule like
#       /feed        /feed.html      301
# creates an infinite loop: /feed → /feed.html → (Cloudflare) → /feed → …
# ERR_TOO_MANY_REDIRECTS. Cloudflare already serves /feed from feed.html, so
# rules of that shape are not just harmful, they are unnecessary.
#
# Two rules follow from this:
#   1. never point a rule at a .html target — write the extensionless path,
#      so the visitor takes one hop instead of two
#   2. list both /x and /x.html as sources, because Cloudflare serves the
#      extensionless form directly and our rule would otherwise miss it
#
# Cloudflare Pages serves /404.html for anything unmatched, and a missing
# hashed asset must 404 rather than fall through to HTML — so no SPA catch-all.
#
# The rules retire duplicate surfaces. GeoHub had six video pages, six
# discovery pages, four business pages and three payment pages; each retired
# URL now 301s to the one canonical page for that job, carrying a ?tab= hint
# so the target opens on the right section (gh-tabs.js reads it).

# ── Video: reels · watch · live · channel → videos ──
/reels               /videos?tab=reels          301
/reels.html          /videos?tab=reels          301
/watch               /videos?tab=watch          301
/watch.html          /videos?tab=watch          301
/live                /videos?tab=live           301
/live.html           /videos?tab=live           301
/channel             /videos?tab=channel        301
/channel.html        /videos?tab=channel        301

# ── Discovery: explore · place-feed · place-updates · reviews → places ──
